<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/docs/Snippet.abide'
import Pool from './Pool.abide'
import poolSource from './Pool.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import downloadsSource from '#src/downloads.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`onStart` runs before the first request">
        <Example
            id="start-hook"
            title="`onStart(fn)` — work the first request cannot race"
            summary="A CRM's database pool, connected before the app answers anything. Reload the page and both times stay where the boot put them."
            files={{
                'Pool.abide': poolSource,
            }}
        >
            <Pool />
        </Example>

        <p><code>onStart</code> takes the <strong>next step</strong> as an argument rather than
        being notified that a step happened. That is the whole difference: work before <code>await
        start()</code> is work the first request cannot race, and work after it is work that knows
        the app is up.</p>

        <p>A listener cannot promise either. It fires alongside the thing it is about, and every
        ordering question it raises has to be answered by a second mechanism.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>before <code>await start()</code></td><td>the app cannot answer yet</td></tr>
                <tr><td>after <code>await start()</code></td><td>it can, and this knows it</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a wrap that <strong>throws</strong></td><td>fails hard; the server does not come up</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Failing hard is the only useful answer to a failed migration. An app that starts anyway
        is an app serving requests against a schema it did not get.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`onStop` closes what `onStart` opened, after the last request">
        <Snippet source={poolSource} path="Pool.abide" from={['onStop(']} />

        <p><code>onStop</code> mirrors it. <code>await stop()</code> first, then tear down what you
        opened — so a connection is closed after the last request that could have used it, not
        during. A running page cannot show you that, and a test can: the one on
        <a href={url('/ship/call-your-app-from-a-test')}>Tests</a> stops the app and finds the pool
        closed.</p>

        <p>A served app runs it however the process is told to end: <code>exit</code> in its shell,
        a <code>SIGTERM</code> from <code>docker stop</code> or a deploy, <code>Ctrl-C</code>. A
        rejection nobody awaited or a throw nothing caught reaches <code>onError</code> first and
        then stops the app the same way, exiting with 1 so a supervisor restarts a clean process. A
        second signal while it is stopping ends it at once.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Every hook hands back the way off">
        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`const off = onStart(warmCaches)
off()`} />

        <p>That is what makes these registrable from whatever server block owns the thing they are
        about. A hook belongs to the route of the file that called it: <code>onStart</code> and
        <code>onStop</code> wrap the whole process wherever they are written, and a section's
        <code>layout.abide</code> filing <code>middleware</code> or <code>onError</code> guards that
        section alone. The root layout is the right home for the app-wide ones because its route is
        every route.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/configure-the-app')}>Config</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A root rung answers what no route does">
        <p><code>middleware</code> filed at the root route runs before routing, so a rung there can
        answer an address no page or handler claims, and hand everything else on with
        <code>next()</code>. These docs serve every page as markdown that way:</p>

        <Snippet source={downloadsSource} path="src/downloads.abide" from={['middleware(async (next']} />

        <p>It uses the same response helpers everything else does, there being no second set.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/answer-with-something-other-than-json')}>Response
        types</a> · <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/app/configure-the-app')}>Config</a> — <code>onConfig</code>,
            registered the same way</li>
            <li><a href={url('/app/tell-a-load-balancer-you-are-healthy')}>Health</a> —
            <code>onHealth</code>, registered the same way</li>
            <li><a href={url('/reference/configuration')}>Configuration</a> — every hook in one
            table</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
